A||||11||fotofrank||Duke will not run right||||9||
Z||000000||fotofrank||11-16-2001||02:57 PM||||My '84 DUKE has aslow idle, no power accelerating, back fires on acceleration, and misses badly. I have tried new plugs to no evale. The EGR has been disconnected and the timing changed but to no good. I get no codes and feel that it is an ignition problem or vaccum leak. Possible immisson stuff. What do you say? I can not use the car as it is. No "get a V6" comments please!<P>------------------<BR>Peace and Happiness<BR>Frank and his fotoflash, an '84 SE 2M4<BR>http://www.homestead.com/fotofrank/foto1.html||||reg||9
Z||000001||V8Dreamer||11-16-2001||03:33 PM||||I had a similar problem in my Grand Am and it turned out to be the map sensor.....I think?  I'm not sure on this one, but if you have one laying around, throw it in and see....||||reg||1
Z||000002||Tigger||11-16-2001||03:37 PM||||Could be the IAC valve.  Common for gunk to build up, spray out with carb cleaner||||reg||1
Z||000003||johnt671||11-16-2001||03:40 PM||||I would do a good tuneup if it's been awhile.  In addition to plugs replace plug wires, cap and rotor, and air and fuel filters. Also clean the throttle body good, paying special attention to the opening at about 7 o'clock on the throttle body as this is the idle control valve and air passage.  See where this leaves you.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Pheagey||11-16-2001||04:24 PM||||Yea, try a general tune up and througth cleaning (worked for me anyways). Also run a lil dry gas throught it a few times....<P>------------------<BR>'88 coupe, 5 speed.<BR>"The car formerly know as Dj's Fiero."||||reg||1
Z||000005||fotofrank||11-16-2001||05:26 PM||||I tried new plugs, distributor cap and rotor. Disconnected the vac hose from the EGR. Tonight I'll disconnect the MAP sensor ,replace the PCV valve and valve cover breather. I wil also replace the TPI with one from an older TB I have. Wish me luck...<P>------------------<BR>Peace and Happiness<BR>Frank and his fotoflash, an '84 SE 2M4<BR>http://www.homestead.com/fotofrank/foto1.html||||reg||8
Z||000006||troydeb||11-16-2001||07:11 PM||||where is the IAC valve?||||reg||5
Z||000007||Tigger||11-16-2001||07:26 PM||||The IAC valve is on the throttle body under the aircleaner-it's on the drivers side of the throttle body.  There's an electrical plug connected to it.||||reg||1
Z||000008||fotofrank||11-16-2001||08:46 PM||||Tried cleaning the IAC already... no dice.||||reg||9
Z||000009||watts||11-17-2001||04:50 AM||||You pulled the vac line to the EGR valve - Ok, did you actually pull the valve out and make sure it's not being pinned open with gunk?  That'll cause a HUGE vac leak that's a pig to track down.  It's a quicky & easy job. <P>After it's out, fill it up with solvent and see how fast it leaks past the pintle.  It should be nothing, or a barely noticable trickle.<BR><P>------------------<BR>87/88 V6 5spd Notchback Hybrid<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/watts_sig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000010||SteveJ||11-17-2001||11:11 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by fotofrank:<BR><B>I tried new plugs, distributor cap and rotor.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>. . .but not the plug wires? Missing, backfire,and bad idle can all be caused by bad wires.||||reg||1
Z||000011||fotofrank||11-17-2001||03:33 PM||||I think its fixed. Found one bad orat least quwstionable plug wire(#4) and finally through a code for the MAP. Replaced both and retimed the car . All seems to be well for a short (less than a mile)test run. Thanks for the suggestions.<P>------------------<BR>Peace and Happiness<BR>Frank and his fotoflash, an '84 SE 2M4<BR>http://www.homestead.com/fotofrank/foto1.html||||reg||10
